"""Ashenvale dungeons: speeches that now carry the place their old accept line used to name."""

STORY = {
    'moonwell_mosshide': ("There's an old gloomcap at the heart of the spread, deep in Moonwell Hollow, north of Astranaar, the size of a house, and the Sentinels call it Mosshide.\n\nMosshide isn't the corruption. It was there before, longer than the Moonwell's lamps, longer than the Sentinels, and I've found it on a chart drawn before anyone in Astranaar was born. It's the anchor. Whatever came into this Hollow needed something already rooted and already old to hang itself on, and it found the oldest thing down there and used it.\n\nCut it down, and the rot loses its hold. I've argued with myself about this for a fortnight, and I've lost every round of it.\n\nIt won't go quietly. It has no reason to, and nothing was ever asked of it.", None),
    'moonwell_maelwyn': ("Pennarin takes a long time to start.\n\nMaelwyn Blightbloom was a Moonwell keeper. She held this post before me. She taught me the lamps, and most of what I know about that water.\n\nNow she's the Blight's flower. That's the Sentinels' phrase, and I haven't been able to improve on it. She's at the Moonwell itself, at the bottom of Moonwell Hollow, north of Astranaar, and as long as she stands in that water it won't run clean. End her there.\n\nShe still has the face I remember. I want you to have been told that rather than discover it. People have gone down not knowing and come back up unable to say why they hesitated. Don't let it slow your hand. Do it for her. I've thought about that phrasing a long while, and I mean it exactly.", None),
    'barrows_rimebone_cull': ("My name's Veyra. I scout the Barrows for the post, and I want them quiet, and I mean that literally.\n\nThat hill sings. It isn't loud. You have to be on the north wall at the right hour, and once you've heard it you hear it every night after. That's my problem, not yours.\n\nIt's the Hunt's old song, and it keeps being sung because the Hunt-King's road is still being kept. The rimebone hounds keep it, and the wights keep it. They walk the Hunt-King's road through the Howling Barrows, a route that hasn't changed in centuries, and the walking is what holds the song together. Break the route, and the song has nothing carrying it.\n\nThey're dead things in a cold hole, and it isn't complicated. Go in with friends, and come out with the job done.", None),
    'groms_felguard_overseer': ("The Bone Hall in Grom's Rest, east of here in the Demon Wastes, is the last gate before the Inner Sanctum, and a Felguard Overseer holds it. Here's why he's mine rather than Grimtotem's.\n\nAn overseer answers to somebody. That's the entire function. He isn't there to fight. He's there so that whatever's in the Sanctum doesn't have to speak to a garrison. And a thing that answers to somebody wears proof of it.\n\nBring me his collar. If the Legion has a hand in Ashenvale, its fingerprints are on that collar: the make, the marks, who had the authority to put it on him.\n\nI've been building this case off stamped plate and shipping routes for a year. A collar finishes it. That's one object between me and being able to name the hand.", None),
    'groms_lord_nightmare': ("The throne is at the heart of Grom's Rest, east in the Demon Wastes, and all of this has been feeding the thing that sits on it.\n\nThat's the shape of the whole place, and it took me the full year to see it. The satyrs, the imps, the hounds, the bindings in the eastern hall, none of that was a garrison defending a position. It was a household keeping something alive. Call it an eldritch horror, because the precise words would take an hour.\n\nKill it, and the throne is empty, and Ashenvale stops paying for it.\n\nI've got no better advice than this, and I've thought hard about whether to give it at all. Don't look at it directly. And don't die, which I'm aware isn't advice. I've got nothing else, and I'd rather say so than invent something.", None),
    'bfd_garrison': ("I'm Lookout Taelanor. Come up here, where I can watch the water while we talk.\n\nThere's a flooded temple under the Zoram cliffs, and the naga have been going in and out of it for weeks. It's a garrison, not a raid, and that distinction is the whole of my report,  A raid comes, takes, and goes. A garrison arrives and stays and gets reinforced, and you only know which you're looking at by counting.\n\nSo I've been counting. I've counted them going in. I haven't counted them coming out. That's the part that frightens me, and I've had three weeks to find a better explanation, and I haven't got one.\n\nThin the warriors before it empties onto the Strand. Go down in company. Whatever's holding that many naga in one hole won't be a pleasant surprise.", None),
    'bfd_hydra_fangs': ("Here's the second thing, and this one's actually professional.\n\nThe murloc hydras down in the flooded temple under the Zoram cliffs shed their fangs, and a hydra fang will cut a rope under tension and never go dull. Every boatwright on this coast has heard the claim, and about half of them think it's a story.\n\nIt isn't a story. I've had one for two years. I've cut standing rigging with it, under load, at sea, and it's exactly as sharp now as the day I took it off the thing. I don't know why. I've asked a druid and I've asked a goblin and got two confident answers that disagreed.\n\nBring me a handful for the rack. Then I'll have a tool rack worth showing off, and I'll sharpen nothing for a year, and that's the whole of my ambition this season.", None),
    'bfd_tide_callers': ("Two of the deeper masters hold the inner temple of the Deeps, under the Zoram cliffs, and they aren't a pair.\n\nLorgus Jett commands the tide. Old Serra'kis commands what the tide has swallowed. They're separate offices, and it took me a long while to understand that the temple has two of them on purpose. One brings the water in. The other keeps whatever comes with it. A garrison isn't arranged that way. A temple is, and it's been running that way since long before the naga we're fighting were born.\n\nYou'll have to go through both. There's no route that takes one and leaves the other.\n\nIt isn't a job for fewer than a full party. I wouldn't go down there with less, and I've been watching that water for three weeks.", None),
}
